A||||3||Richard Parnell||V8 Fuel pump question||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||10-06-2000||02:24 PM||||I'm in the midst of a V8 conversion on my 87 SC.  My buddy who is actaully doing most of the work since he is in the business and has done 4 other Fiero V8 conversions, wants to get rid of the electric fuel pump in the gas tank and use a regular fuel pump mounted at the normal spot on the engine.  He did this on his own Fiero w/350 after the stock Fiero pump burned out 4 times on his car.  This happened even with a regulator in the line.  I'm inclined to go along with him since there seems to be room for the chevy fuel pump to clear everything when the motor is installed.  Does anyone have any feed back and/or experience with this?  Archie, Ogre?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Archie||10-06-2000||03:08 PM||||Well the burning up fuel pumps is a pretty uncommon thing, unless he was running the tank empty.<P>I've been at this nearly 15 years and have never use the stock Chevy mechanical Fuel Pump. <P>So I always recommend that you use the stock Fiero 4 cyl. pump on a carburated car. If you are using fuel injection the mechanical pump won't kep up with demands anyway.<P>However, If you are going to use the mechanical pump remember that those pumps are no too good at pulling fuel up out of the gas tank and you really should have the feed line coming from the bottom of the tank. And remember that the Mechanical pump is gravity fed and with the machanical pump mounted to the engine, the bottom of the pump is about 3 inches above the bottom of the fuel tank. So I would wonder if you will be able to draw the last 3" of gas out of the tank.<P>Remember that the right side Fiero axle shaft runs right by the area where the mechanical fuel pump will be located and I would worry about the axle shaft contacting it throughout the suspension travel. <P>These are all reasons that I've always used, and recommended that people use, the in tank pump.<P>Good luck.<BR>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||10-06-2000||03:21 PM||||Thanks Archie , I will talk with him some more about this.  I did say to him that I had not heard of anyone else having that problem with the electric pumps.  Perhaps he isn't setting up the regulator properly.  I just want to be sure to get the thing set up right and also be able to have the setup so I can replace things easily if necessary.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Spyder||10-06-2000||07:38 PM||||I unhooked the power to my old in tank fuel pump and used an electric external fuel pump to pull the fuel through.  I did this so I wouldn’t ever have to drop the tank (x-frame) and so I could add an extra fuel cell. I don't think I would use the stock Chevy pump though.||||reg||1
